  },
  CollectionHall_124141_Title = {Text = "Home"},
  CollectionHall_124142_Desc = {
    Text = "Let us call upon the name of the Divine Realm that rises from the sea—Lemuria, Lemuria."
  },
  CollectionHall_124142_LockTip = {
    Text = "Unlocks after completing Astral Reign 4-16"
  },
  CollectionHall_124142_Title = {Text = "Lemuria"},
  CollectionHall_124224_LockTip = {
    Text = "Unlocks after clearing any stage of Multiverse Link \"Baptism of Silver and Blood\""
  },
  CollectionHall_124224_Title = {
    Text = "Mouchette's Otherworld Investigation Record"
  },
  CollectionHall_124395_Desc = {
    Text = "On the most agonizing yet fulfilling day of her life, Lotan's blades raged like a storm against the god before her. In the storm, her greatblades and Leviathan's planetary bulk shattered together, splinter by splinter.\nShe gifted the blood-drenched Crown to the knightly order's slave. In exchange, she asked for but one thing: a new weapon.\nAwed and terrified, the new king ordered the bones of Leviathan harvested and forged into a pair of peerless bone blades. Wrought and quenched day and night by the Six Kingdoms' finest blacksmiths, the remains of a dead deity were reborn in the hands of its slayer.\nA worthy rival once witnessed these undying blades. His warhammer shattered Lotan's armor and her bone blades. Spiderweb cracks raced across the blade, ivory shards scattering into the crimson air—just as they had on the day she struck down Leviathan. He thought he'd found an opening. Then he watched her rise again before him. The blade that should have been broken drove through his chest, drinking his blood, devouring his flesh. From the fractures, new bone erupted—sharper, denser, splintering outward like wild thorns in the wasteland. He felt, with perfect clarity, his life being chewed and swallowed by the bone-forged blade.\nAs it turned out, the star-devouring beast had never truly died. From the moment their pact was sealed, It had become one with her. Each time Lotan raises her blades, bone spines erupt along the edges, their razor tips shrieking in cruel triumph.\nIt is her weapon. She is its stride. From beginning to end, she and it have shared the same hunger. Bound as one, they are undying and insatiable.\nAnd the eternal battlefield is their only home."
  },
  CollectionHall_124395_Title = {
    Text = "Undying Hungerbone"
  },
  CollectionHall_124397_Desc = {
    Text = "Many years later, facing the prostrate seafarers in prayer, Murphy: Fauxborn would recall that distant afternoon when \"Mother\" told her the story of the blue-ringed octopus.\n\nThe relic in the jar was soft and dazzling, with strange, ethereal blue floating in the water, enticing her young self to draw near involuntarily.\n\nMeanwhile, Miryam stroked her head, compelling her to look at the dim corner of the cabin with an undeniable force—there lay the lifeless body of a seafarer, slumped on the ground, presenting a pallid emptiness as if drained of all toxin.\n\n\"Remember, the most tranquil and deadly in the ocean is often the most beautiful.\"\n\nThe cold light reflected off the glass jar illuminated her compassionate silhouette, and Murphy: Fauxborn felt a fear wrapped in beauty.\n\n\nTime passed quietly in rhythm with the stars, and Murphy: Fauxborn remained as willful as ever, yet she learned to be silent and observant.\n\n\"Lemuria, Lemuria...\"\n\nUntil, upon the altar, that usually calm and gentle face gradually became tainted by madness, she finally realized that she had never truly known Miryam.\n\nThe hand that caressed her head, her gentle mother, could also deliver the harshest punishment to the enemies of the Divine Realm.\n\nAt that moment, the fear sealed deep within her memory surged forth, ultimately crystallizing into a cold realization—\nher kindness was merely a guise of the tentacle, and the beautiful blue ring transformed from a gentle exterior was the very toxin that could seal one's fate with blood.\n\n\nTo this day, the fleeting deity crowned Murphy: Fauxborn.\n\nShe looked down at the crawling seafarers, meeting the glimmer of absurd hope in Miryam's gaze, as if she once again saw the small, trembling self that had stared at the beautiful creature before the glass jar."
  },
  CollectionHall_124397_Title = {
    Text = "Blue-Ringed Toxin"
  },
  CollectionHall_124400_Desc = {
    Text = "An unnamed plague devoured towns and villages; warmongers slaughtered freely amid the chaos; a symphony of iron and blood boiled into madness.\nOut of fear, or exploiting fear.\nFear is an invisible enemy, an ancient enemy, a lethal enemy.\nHe was an unworthy knight — he had been defeated by fear.\nYet the vow he had once solemnly sworn still echoed in his ears, year after year, passing through a homeland already reduced to nothing.\n\"I shall treat the weak with mercy, and face the strong with courage.\"\nThe night concealed the footprints of wrongdoers; floods of screams and weeping drowned the streets and alleys; the crowds scattered like panicked sheep.\n\"I shall oppose the wicked without reservation, and fight for those who cannot fight for themselves.\"\nThe lance tip was stained with blood, the silver shield corroded with rust; the shadow smiled in satisfaction; crows circled overhead; a demonic gleam flickered in his pupils.\n\"I shall help those who need my help.\"\n\"It's him, he's come.\" The people murmured in hushed voices, fear twisting their faces. \"The hollow monster, the ghost of vengeance, returned from the depths of death to drag his enemies into the black mire of hatred.\"\nIt was him. He had not forgotten — he had never forgotten.\nWhy persist? If all things must eventually perish, even glory and faith.\nWhy retreat? Since no matter how fate mocks, a person can only submit and kneel.\nIs it only to be loyal to loyalty itself, to dedicate oneself to dedication?\nThe last knight of the duchy asked in vain; his soul was as desolate as his broken body; the lingering filth of fear flowed unchecked, gathering into a vast ocean of destruction.\nYet the hollow chest remained silent, and the lost answer was still nowhere to be found."
  },
  CollectionHall_124400_Title = {
    Text = "Days Gone By Like a Shadow"
  },
  CollectionHall_124404_Desc = {
    Text = "That was the second year after my father and I had moved to Shanghai. Evelyne's tearful complaints in her letter left me distraught, and I had already resolved to part ways with her.\n\nShanghai is a land draped in silk and ebony, where every vulture has sensed the chaos stirring in its slumber. Here, paper notes trade for their weight in gold, and whoever commands the shipping routes can move mountains of wealth at a pittance.\n\nYet Evelyne—perhaps driven by the neurosis of her obscure and tainted lineage—clung to her so-called ancestral message. She believed that in the East, sinister deities are worshipped by Their offshoots; that like deep-sea fish, They dangle swaying, delicious bait to lure prey into a slow, imperceptible feast.\n\nShe was wrong. Shanghai bears not the faintest tang of the brine. There is only the pervasive scent of white magnolia—so cloyingly sweet it drowns the senses in equal parts rapture and terror. The very magnolias that ensnared both my father and me.\n\n...Then, I saw her. The instant the woman named \"Xu\" appeared, I knew I could never tear myself away from this ancient soil.\n\nShe made me want to pull her close; she made me want to make her mine; she made me want to @3. She made made MADE Me mE want WANT wAnTwantWANT to SLICE HER, CUT HER, HACK HER forever forEVERFOREVERforever... @2@3@2!!\n\nAh... Why am I here? What am I supposed to take away? Who... am I? It no longer matters. She invited me, and now I sit at the table in that grand mansion.\n\nMy father—as mad now as I—wears a docile smile. A dazzling array of exquisite side dishes fills the table, though the lidded metal tray seems... out of place.\n\nXu sits in the sunset, a golden halo rimming her silhouette against the dying light. Slowly, she lowers her black fan. \n\nShe looks like Evelyne, like my childhood playmate, like a fleeting lover from some hazy romance... like my mother. Her face is as serene and exquisitely sculpted as a gilded temple idol.\n\nI hear no slurping. I smell no copper tang of blood from my father. I only stare, entranced, at that face—then at the pale, slender hand that reaches out, lifts the lid, and selects a choice piece of meat. With two thin wooden sticks, she pinches it up, raising it toward me.\n\nShe calls to me. The rendered animal fat drips onto the corner of my mouth. \"Young sir,\" she says, \"you should eat something.\"\n\nObedient and numb, I open my mouth. The magnolia is already in bloom; it rustles softly, swaying down toward me. \n\n—\"Journal of the Nameless.\""
  },
  CollectionHall_124404_Title = {
    Text = "Magnolia's Lure"
  },
  CollectionHall_124405_Desc = {
    Text = "With reason as a guiding light, one can dispel the mist of life.\n\nOnce upon a time, Mouchette had heard such words.\n\nPerhaps it was a technician from Thoreau Company casually expressing this sentiment while debugging a mist lamp?\nOr maybe it was a quip from a passerby during a visit to the Filingen restaurant?\n\nMouchette had forgotten; all she could clearly remember was the habitual social smile on her face when she heard those words, and the sarcasm hidden behind that smile.\n\n\"Guiding light?\nHa, what an old-fashioned relic.\nPeople use flashlights these days.\"\n\nShe wasn't unaware that it was just a metaphor; she simply scoffed at the idea.\n\nBecause one day, she would die.\nAnd after death, what reason, what mist, would remain—she would be completely liberated from this world.\n\nThat was a reasonable and inevitable conclusion.\nAll she needed to do was enjoy the process before this \"inevitability\" arrived.\n\nBut no one had ever told her that \"inevitability\" could also encounter \"unexpectedness.\"\n\nSimilar mist, similar predicaments, yet there were no mist lamps, no investigation teams, none of the familiar things.\nHer axe could sever the monsters of the world, but it could not cut through the mist born of her own loneliness and confusion.\n\nCan she go back?\nWill she go back?\nWhen can she go back...\n\nOne question after another surfaced from her heart, like an invisible deep water, slowly engulfing her soul.\nAt this moment, the only thought in her mind, the entirety of her thoughts, was—\nMaintain reason.\n\nDon't go insane!\nDon't go insane!\nDon't go insane!\n\nWhether facing the mist of that world, the mist of this world, or the mist within her heart.\n\nFor the sake of returning once more."
  },
  CollectionHall_124405_Title = {
    Text = "Light of Intellect"
  },
  CollectionHall_124407_Desc = {
    Text = [[
On the day of her seventh birthday, her father rested a spinning wheel called "Moirae" upon her lap.

It bore the name of three Goddesses of Fate long forsaken by prayer, yet the wheel itself was sleek and new, its copper axle gleaming with a metallic sheen, cold to the touch and heavy with silence.

From that day forth, Arachne took up the wheel of fate-not as a choice, but as a sentence. For her unintended words always came true in time, and she could not turn away from the weight of such a burden.

Day after day, she wove without respite, watching the threads of fate coil silently around the intricate wheel, weaving for the world the tremor of a first kiss, the neighing of warhorses, the first cry of a newborn, and the jagged epitaphs carved into stone.

Among thousands of tangled filaments, she selected the "better" paths. She beckoned the dark tide of plague to ebb; she spared history from the consuming flames of war; she allowed civilizations to rebuild from the ruins...

Yet, to sustain that web stretching across time and space, she also wove the strands leading to madness, betrayal, or eternal night.

"What is truly better?"

The silent wheel offered no answer. It only continued to spin.

Virtue and vice, cradles and coffins-all flickered alternately along the same thread.

Young as she was then, she did not yet know the shape of her own fate, yet at the end of countless threads, she had already glimpsed the world's end.

"If the world cannot escape its ruin..."

"No. I love this world."

Arachne gazed upon the wheel, letting the unspoken thought dissolve into silence.

"Then... what of my own fate?"

She murmured to herself, with only hollow echoes wandering between the four walls.

Countless civilizations rose and crumbled upon the web of fate. Pyramids slumbered beneath the sand; spacecraft streaked across seas of stars; languages changed like the turning of seasons; and the names of gods were interred in the dust of forgotten lexicons...

And she sat there always, turning the wheel in her hands, watching humanity reenact its cycle of hope and devastation.

Until, at last, a gentle voice that transcended the eternal prison pierced the silence of the ages.

"Sister..."

She finally withdrew her gaze from the apocalypse, looking down at Nefraea beside her. In those eyes, there was no judgment, no fear, only complete trust, like a blank sheet of paper awaiting the first stroke of ink.

In that moment, Arachne finally understood: love and duty, hatred and ruin-all were the same in the eyes of fate.

And so she gathered those memories she had once snipped and discarded-the pain, the regret, the loss that should have come but never did-and braided them into a single cord. Then, as the heaviest yet most tender of gifts, she tied it around Nefraea's wrist.

"Our bond?" Arachne smiled. "It is fate."

Arachne no longer needed to see her own fate clearly.

For in the moment she picked up the wheel at age seven, she had already chosen the direction of her future.

And the wheel of fate would go on turning. It would bear witness to the battle she was about to enter-a battle with no final curtain call.]]
  },
  CollectionHall_124407_Title = {
    Text = "Rota Fortunae"
  },
  CollectionHall_124410_Desc = {
    Text = "Eat eat eat eat...\nDelusions born of bewitchment.\nCrush crush crush crush...\nIncoherent murmurs of the abyss.\nDestroy! Destroy!! DESTROY!!! DESTROY!!!!\nUnstoppable, unbridled madness.\n\"Vortice hungry.\"\nThe girl gorged on chunks of flesh still dripping with blood. No matter how much she ate, the emptiness in her stomach refused to fade.\n\"Vortice thirsty.\"\nThe girl gulped the rank, rotting seawater. No matter how much she drank, the parched burning in her throat would not relent.\n\"Vortice sleepy.\"\nThe girl hit herself to stay awake. No matter how long she slept, the exhaustion in her mind never lifted.\nFor only pure destruction can appease the Thing within.\n\"But... Vortice says NO!\"\nThe girl defied the siren call of fate. She bound herself in specialized steel—a cage she can never shed.\nShe forged herself into an unbreakable prison; she used her own existence as the shackles to imprison the madness from the deep.\n\"Vortice, lock the bad thing!\"\nAnd so, the great whirlpool Moskstraumen, which appeared in AF 1■■ and claimed countless lives, fell into absolute silence at the end of AF 31■."
  },
  CollectionHall_124410_Title = {
    Text = "Imprisoned Madness"
  },
  CollectionHall_124413_Desc = {
    Text = "Besides dry research papers, the bookshelf held a few children's books that looked rather charming. <Italic:Cinderella. Snow White. The Little Mermaid. Rapunzel...> Why their owner had kept these here—there was no longer any way of knowing.\n\nThose stories wove a world nothing like reality. \"Prince.\" \"Princess.\" \"Love.\" These words set the young girl's heart aflutter.\n\nCountless lights glimmered across the night sky, spilling down to earth as though gathering into a river of flowing radiance.\n\nIn the falling moonlight, the girl quietly rose onto her tiptoes, pretending to step into a pair of glass slippers, transparent as air. As though she had always been waiting—for this deep, lonely, endless night to finally break into dawn...\""
  },
  CollectionHall_124413_Title = {
    Text = "Muddied Fairy Tale"
  },
  CollectionHall_124414_Desc = {
    Text = "The old witch drifted between merchant ships, her figure always cloaked in the shadows of her tattered robe. The compass, she said, would guide people exactly where they were meant to go. The sailors laughed and jeered at her herb-scented nonsense, tossed down five Leonian Florins, and walked away with the exquisite brass trinket.\nLater, they docked at a small harbor town—one that would eventually lose its name.\nPontos loved this story. The compass's owner was charmed by Pontos.\nAfter all, no one could dislike Pontos—he was humorous, cheerful, as carefree as a passing breeze.\nGifted to his beloved nephew, it was meant to be a wonderful keepsake, a key to awaken his yearning for the distant town. Yet, it concealed the boy from him. Pontos would not allow it, nor would he ever permit his nephew's life to take any other course.\nYears passed, and as Pontos wandered through countless moonlit nights, searching for the figure who should have returned to Ebwynnos, this was exactly what he thought.\nOh right, that futile, protective spell had worn off at last. Pontos could finally bring him back.\nPontos knew the old tale well—that the compass guides people exactly where they are meant to go. What he failed to realize, however, was that just as every ship charts its own course, different travelers will inevitably choose different paths."
  },
  CollectionHall_124414_Title = {
    Text = "Compass to False North"
  },
  CollectionHall_124415_Desc = {
    Text = "\"Won't you buy a match?\" the girl whispered, her hands and feet red with cold. It was a winter night. \"Just one. Please.\"\n\n\"I'm sorry, little one. I won't be buying your matches,\" said the person in white, bowing just slightly, blotting out the winter moon. \"But I have something to sell to you.\"\n\n\"But I don't have any money...\"\n\n\"That's all right. Trust in the fairy's blessing, and you'll be selling your matches in no time.\"\n\nThe fairy in white pressed a sweet into the girl's hand, then leaned close to her ear.\n\n\"Do you see that middle-aged woman across the street, the one in the fur cloak? Take hold of her hem and cry as hard as you can. Say: Kind lady, it's so terribly cold, and my grandmother is gravely ill—if she can't get one good meal she'll die of sickness by morning. Please, I'm begging you, give me a little money.\"\n\n\"But Grandmother left me a long, long time ago...\"\n\n\"Better still! Just try to remember how that felt, and it'll make the crying more convincing...\"\n\n\"And if she's still stingy and won't open her purse, I have another trick. A woman out wandering the streets on a night like New Year's Eve—her home life is surely an unhappy one. So you add: Grandmother is the only family I have, and tonight of all nights, on New Year's Eve, no one wants to be all alone. Please, beautiful lady, only three pennies, so I can sell out my matches early and go home to be with Grandmother again.\"\n\n\"But a match costs one penny...\"\n\n\"The other two pennies—one is my commission, and the last one is an offering to it.\" Caraboo gave the wand in her hand a little shake, and the sleeping white grubs flowed like ripples over water.\n\n\"Don't think too hard about it. Just trust in the fairy's blessing, and this trick will work every time. Now remember: Don't let go of her hem until she's got the money out. And if it fails, we'll simply go and find the next lonely rich woman. Don't you worry; in a world like this one, there are more than enough rich women with spare coin to feed the two of us.\"\n\nWhy a trick that worked every time should ever fail—no one bothered to look into that anymore. In any case, after updating the script one hundred and eighty-two times, the little girl did sell out her matches.\n\nThere is no Fairy Godmother in the real world, but a counterfeit fairy saved the poor, pitiful girl from freezing to death all the same.\n\nHow wonderful. How wonderful indeed."
  },
  CollectionHall_124415_Title = {
    Text = "Serene Truth"
  },
  CollectionHall_124844_Desc = {
    Text = "A mysterious girl hailing from Temurahn. By her own account, she was once a mere university student until a \"fateful romantic encounter\" thrust her into the role of vanguard for the A.U.G.E.—<Italic:Agency for the Unidentified Great Emergency>.\nDuring the Sect of Astral Dawn's \"Mist Disaster,\" Mouchette was unexpectedly summoned to the realm of Kadath. There, through a sacred ritual of Silver and Blood, she underwent a profound baptism—transcending her human limits to become an Awakener and ultimately bringing the \"Mist Disaster\" to its end.\nThough she remains a traveler immersed in a world where she does not truly belong, Mouchette continues to search for a path home. Yet, she is equally captivated by the shifting tides of this realm, driven by a yearning to witness \"the end of the world.\""
