  },
  CollectionHall_130219_Desc = {
    Text = "This is an island marked on no nautical chart.\nSince its birth, the virus known as \"civilization\" has never set foot upon its pristine shores.\nThere is only sand, jungle, an endless stretch of sea... and one small girl.\n.........\n\"Papa! Mama! Fish!\"\n\"Whistle—\"\nTwo dolphins glide leisurely to the girl's side, their cries melodic and clear.\nOne lifts her onto its back; the other takes the stunned fish from her hands and swallows it in a single gulp.\n\"Whistle—\"\n\"Hehe. Vortice! Strong!\"\nLike a child untouched by the world, the girl flexes her small arms, beaming at the praise from \"Papa\" and \"Mama.\"\n\"Today, sun! Good!\"\n\"Vortice! Papa! Mama! Swim!\"\nAt her words, the dolphin carrying her lets out a soft trill and begins a slow circle around the little island.\nWarm sunlight spills over the girl, dispelling the damp chill that has lingered for days. A gentle breeze rustles through her hair, carrying the ocean's lazy breath.\nLaughter, splashing, playful cries. On this isolated isle, there are no burdens of the modern world—only the most primal, purest joy.\nAs always, the two creatures and one girl spend another simple, carefree day.\n.........\nUntil the endless calling rises up and devours that tranquility whole.\n\"Come back... Come back...\"\n\"@1 needs you... @2...@4... needs you...\""
  },
  CollectionHall_130219_Title = {
    Text = "The Faraway Eden"
  },
  CollectionHall_130220_Desc = {
    Text = "There was a dandelion. Smaller than any bird, lighter than any breeze.\n\nWhen the dandelion passed over the first place, a wasteland, it asked the tumbleweeds scattered across the earth: <Italic:how do you propagate?>\nThe tumbleweeds answered: \"We let our seeds drift to the winds, until they find soil worth taking root in.\"\n\nA wanderer who drifts has no home. The dandelion sighed, and caught another breeze away.\n\nWhen the dandelion passed over the second place, a desert, it asked the cacti dotting the sand: <Italic:how do you survive?\n>\nThe cacti answered: \"We drive our roots deep underground, drawing from water that is scarce and precious.\"\nLife endures with such stubbornness, even in the harshest of places. The dandelion turned this over in its thoughts, and moved on.\n\nBefore long, the dandelion drifted over a city.\nThrough the dark of night, every light that bloomed across it was a mark of human life.\nAt the end of its long journey, the dandelion's seed was finally ready to fall—down to that waiting earth below."
  },
  CollectionHall_130220_Title = {
    Text = "Fragrant Morphogenesis"
  },
  CollectionHall_130221_Desc = {
    Text = [[
Arachne labored under the shadow of a known futility.

Day after day, she turned the spinning wheel, weaving into its vast, timeless web the joys and griefs of humans, the glory and ruin of empires, and the fleeting spark of poetry and love.

She wrought masterfully-precise, restrained, and gentle.

She discarded the "errant" threads and salvaged the "righteous" ones. And so generation after generation blossomed from decay; civilization after civilization rose from ashes to write itself anew.

Yet the joy once kindled by weaving, the weariness worn into her bones, and her every last tenderness-all had long since withered, ground to nothing beneath the ceaseless turning of her labor.

She had severed her own vulnerability. What remained was will alone, lucid and cold-so that every choice she made might rise from duty, and never from weakness.

The true tragedy, however, lay not in the labor itself, but in her own merciless clarity. She knew that no matter how faithfully she mended and wove, the world would eventually succumb to the pull of the silent void.

Civilizations were but sandcastles before an inevitable, encroaching tide.

Her devotion was a grand performance staged for an empty theater; a vigil to which nothing, and no one, would ever answer.

"Fate has long condemned us to eternal solitude-why should we endure so cruel a sentence?"

"Tear it apart, destroy it... and you will escape this toil..."

She lowered her eyes, gently snipping away those wayward thoughts, then bent once more to her work: weaving, sustaining, suffering, enduring...

All futile struggles and all unsolvable fates had long been woven into this web.

And there, at the center of the web, she beheld herself.]]
  },
  CollectionHall_130221_Title = {
    Text = "Eternal Weave"
