  CollectionHall_132222_Desc = {
    Text = "If wishes could truly come true, Pontos would wish for farewells to announce themselves before they arrived.\nHis parents vanished without a trace. On a quiet afternoon, his sister Leisha hired a rickshaw, which later brought them to a long iron behemoth reeking of diesel, rattling and roaring, until it spat him out at a seaside town.\nLeisha never breathed a single word about their parents' whereabouts. After a fierce argument, he stormed off, rushing into the harbor thick with the stench of brine and fish.\nThe sea folk, with unnaturally wide-set eyes, were peddling bizarre fish dredged from the abyss—depths no human strength could ever reach.\n\"Hey, stay back. Those fish might be cursed, just like the creatures that caught them,\" a passerby warned in a hushed tone.\nNo one wanted to see this handsome, newly arrived young man get mixed up in trouble.\nHe offered his habitual easygoing smile, but his gaze was irresistibly drawn to a dark, decaying lure. Right beneath it lay a gaping maw bristling with sharp teeth, ugly and contorted, curving inward in savage disarray. A phantom voice murmured from the depths of his mind, recounting the life of this dead creature.\n<Italic:Down in the lightless deep, it lured, plundered, and stopped at nothing. Its body was a fortress, a living cage, trapping its offspring, its lovers, its enemies, and its prey.>\n<Italic:All the beautiful things in its life were meant to be fleeting, but it held them all fast. It made its own body their vessel, fusing them in an inseparable, eternal embrace.>\nPontos gave his head a sharp shake, looking around in terror amidst a sharp, ringing noise in his ears.\nThe voice receded like a tide. He dismissed the whole episode as a fleeting delusion, silently mocking his own frayed nerves.\nAnd so, entirely unaware, he began his descent into his preordained fate."
  },
  CollectionHall_132222_Title = {
    Text = "The Living Cage"
  },
  CollectionHall_133709_Desc = {
    Text = "Morning rides meant trotting with local squires to gauge their stance on the Reform Bill; luncheons required trading flattery with high-society matrons. At dinner, there were hollow, endless pre-meal prayers under silver candelabras, and even after the opera, the theater corridors were a gauntlet of ceaseless social pleasantries.\n\n\"Only afternoon tea on the back lawn feels like the only time that's truly mine.\"\n\"You are my first guest for afternoon tea. I’ll let you choose the topic then.\"\n\nSorel lifted a freshly poured cup of black tea.\n\"What would you like to know? Count de Crémay’s stance on the Reform Bill? The Beaumont family’s political gambling? Or perhaps a Viscountess’s secrets?\"\n\"None? You only care about the taste of this pink dessert?\"\nShe took a slow sip, her gaze finally settling on you.\n\"Then, by all means, have a taste.\"\n\"Some things must be experienced firsthand to be truly understood.\"\n\"Wouldn't you agree, my esteemed—Excellency?\"\n\nBlack mist began to seep from the collar and cuffs of the guest's uniform, churning into a mass of dark chaos.\n\"Hehehe... do you really think you can escape your fate?\"\n\"The sun is perfect, and I intend to enjoy my rare moment of leisure first,\" Sorel replied. \"If you wish to sample the season’s finest tea and sponge cake, help yourself. Otherwise, come back when my break is over.\""
  },
  CollectionHall_133709_Title = {
    Text = "Private Afternoon"
  },
  CollectionHall_133710_Desc = {
    Text = "As a top student at Mythag, Ramona operates on pure logic and reason. She is calm, witty, and incredibly reliable.\nYet occasionally, even she falls into quiet reverie when faced with the world’s absolute terror—or its absolute splendor.\nGazing at the endless sea of pink, Ramona fell silent.\nShe knew these cherry blossoms would fade within three weeks, their lush branches soon turning bare and desolate.\nA human life lasts far longer than three weeks or thirty years, yet like these trees, it comes only once.\nThe greatest human illusion is the belief that one will live forever.\nRamona understands this.\nLike the blooming and wilting of flowers, human life and death are ultimately just phenomena of nature.\nRamona understands this, too.\nBut humans always seek to give their existence meaning; that is why every new student enters the gates of Mythag.\nThey are the pioneers against Dissolution, the final line of defense for humanity’s future.\nTheir lives aren't just natural phenomena; even destined to perish, they are actively shaping the course of this world.\nThey fight side by side, weathering every storm together.\nLike two blossoms on the same branch, they bud together, sway in the wind together, and wither together.\nNow, it is time to go back.\nThe one who relies on her—and whom she can rely on in return—is still waiting."
  },
