  CollectionHall_149045_Desc = {
    Text = "\"Put the wreath on properly — this is your knighting ceremony, my dear little knight.\"\nWhen daisies bloomed across the meadows, there were always idle soldiers who, under the pretense of standing guard, would drag the young man along to wander here.\nAn ornament woven from the most common flowers of the Duchy of Aquitaine was gently placed upon golden hair.\n\"My lord, perhaps... we should be training?\"\nThe young man said as much, yet obediently straightened the white wreath on his head.\nThese small, delicate plants always bathed in the light, and in the young man's homeland, they were also a symbol of hope.\nWhen he opened his eyes again, those bright and vivid scenes were gone.\nWar horses were screaming, enemies surged forward with contempt, and amid the thousands of troops, he stood alone.\nThen, the grating clamor ceased abruptly —\nA black tide poured into his soul.\nFollowing it came the tearing sound of a lance piercing flesh, the rush of flowing blood, the wailing of enemies.\nThe scorn on those faces vanished without a trace, replaced by overwhelming fear and retreat.\nAnd the young man only raised his hand mechanically — thrust, withdraw, raise again.\nUntil the last soldier fell.\nDust and smoke swallowed countless faces; the remains of conquerors and conquered alike piled upon the ground, indistinguishable from one another.\nThe shadow coiled around his spine and sneered: \"Keep going. Let your lance fall — just one more time.\"\n\"Between guardian and destroyer, there has always been only a thin line. Now, you have made your choice.\"\nDarkness shrouded the sky, and blood soaked the earth.\nHe looked toward the distant countryside — in just a single night, it had forever lost the colors it once held.\nThe wind lifted the hem of the young man's clothes, and swept across that patch of grass that had somehow survived.\nIn the silent night, in a corner even demons did not know of, a barely perceptible trace of pure white still lingered in the depths of his eyes.\nIt was the last daisy in this land, growing quietly in a place no one cared to notice.\nThough stained with red and its stem bent, it had not yet fallen."
  },
  CollectionHall_149045_Title = {
    Text = "And the Daisies Still Effloresce"
  },
  CollectionHall_149759_Desc = {
    Text = "<Title:Introduction>\nOgier finally admitted that, at his age and with his strength, he was not yet fit to be a knight-protector of the realm.\nIn this world, he never received that crucial word of support.\nAnd so, persuaded by the princess, he returned to his father's domain and lived as an ordinary young nobleman.\n\nWhen the flames of war came, Ogier — unable to cast aside his identity as a knight — rushed back to the royal palace, but it was too late.\nThe princess had destroyed herself in the most brutal way; not one of his familiar comrades had survived — they had held their ground until the very last moment.\nWhen enemy soldiers stormed the already ruined palace, intent on plundering it bare, he could not suppress his rage and grief, and chose to surrender his body to that seductive dark power.\nThe enemies had no time to regroup, and fell at his hands to the last man — yet after his revenge, the young man felt not a shred of relief.\n\nThe pitch-black shadow claimed his body and stained his soul, and in those dazed and hollow days, he committed many wrongs.\n\n— Slaughter, destruction, doing all manner of things that violated his vows... until a ray of sunlight Awakened him from the boundless void.\n\nPerhaps it was not too late to begin regretting now.\nHe still had the chance to take up spear and shield once more, and fight in defense of virtue."
  },
